{
  "fromId": "uo",
  "toId": "u",
  "preamble": "Monophthongization of the diphthong [uo] to a long [uː] merges the diphthong with its close onset. It is a regular change in three unrelated traditions: the early New High German monophthongization turned Middle High German uo into ū (guote > gute, bruoder > Bruder); in Czech the diphthong uo that arose from Old Czech ó became [uː], still written ů (kuoň > kůň); and Kildin Sami monophthongized Proto-Samic *uo to ū in words such as *kuolē 'fish' > кӯлль.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization, Raising",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "German",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Middle High German guote [uə] 'good'",
          "to": "gute [uː]",
          "note": "Early New High German monophthongization (from the 11th century in central German) turned ie, uo, üe into [iː], [uː], [yː]; Bavarian and Alemannic kept the diphthongs."
        },
        {
          "from": "Middle High German bruoder 'brother'",
          "to": "Bruder [ˈbruːdɐ]",
          "note": "Alemannic still has a diphthong (Brüeder)."
        },
        {
          "from": "Middle High German vuoȥ 'foot'",
          "to": "Fuß [fuːs]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Czech",
      "languageFamily": "West Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Czech kuoň (< kóň) 'horse'",
          "to": "kůň [kuːɲ]",
          "note": "Old Czech long ó diphthongized to uo, which then monophthongized to [uː]; the letter ů (u with a small o above) preserves the diphthong spelling. Genitive koně keeps short o."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Czech dóm > duom 'house'",
          "to": "dům [duːm]",
          "note": "Genitive domu."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Kildin Sami",
      "languageFamily": "Samic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Samic *kuolē 'fish'",
          "to": "кӯлль, with ӯ [uː]",
          "note": "Kildin shows ū [uː] for Proto-Samic *uo in these words, while other Samic languages keep a diphthong (Northern Sami guolli)."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Samic *kuoktē 'two'",
          "to": "кӯһт",
          "note": "Same change; compare Northern Sami guokte."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Samic *kuorkë 'crane'",
          "to": "кӯррк",
          "note": "Same change; compare Northern Sami guorga."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Samic *luoktë 'bay'",
          "to": "лӯххт",
          "note": "Same change; compare Northern Sami luokta."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
